---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import GetNotified from '../components/GetNotified.astro';
import { CHURCH } from '../data/site';

const title = `Springboard — October 2026 | ${CHURCH.name}`;
const description = 'Read the October 2026 Springboard newsletter: a letter from Pastor Neill, the October calendar, fellowship, community outreach, and church news.';
const calendarItems = [
  { date: 'October 1', detail: 'Choir Rehearsal — 7:00 PM' },
  { date: 'October 4', detail: 'Sunday School — 10:15 AM; Worship / World Communion Sunday — 11:30 AM; Deacons’ Meeting — 1:00 PM' },
  { date: 'October 8', detail: 'Choir Rehearsal — 7:00 PM' },
  { date: 'October 10', detail: 'Village Wide Yard Sale' },
  { date: 'October 11', detail: 'Sunday School — 10:15 AM; Worship — 11:30 AM; Westminster Guild; BBQ Fundraiser at Hopewell — 5:00–7:00 PM' },
  { date: 'October 15', detail: 'Choir Rehearsal — 7:30 PM' },
  { date: 'October 18', detail: 'Sunday School — 10:15 AM; Worship — 11:30 AM; Fun & Fellowship — Sunshine Bags; Budget & Stewardship Meeting' },
  { date: 'October 22', detail: 'Choir Rehearsal — 7:30 PM' },
  { date: 'October 25', detail: 'Sunday School — 10:15 AM; Worship — 11:30 AM; Coffee & Conversation — Apple vs. Pumpkin; Session Meeting' },
  { date: 'October 31', detail: 'Pastor Neill’s Halloween Party at the Manse — before trick-or-treating' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <PageHero title="Springboard" image="/images/bg-1-scaled.jpg" size="lg" />
  <main>
    <section class="bg-cream py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="text-center mb-12">
          <p class="eyebrow-crimson mb-2">October 2026 Newsletter</p>
          <h1 class="display-xl mb-4">First Presbyterian Church of Montgomery, NY</h1>
          <p class="font-mono text-sm text-ink-soft">Christ Center · Community Driven · Worship</p>
          <a href="/newsletter-september-2026" class="inline-block font-mono text-sm text-crimson hover:underline mt-5">Previous issue: September 2026 →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white p-7 md:p-8 shadow-sm border-t-4 border-crimson">
            <h2 class="eyebrow-navy mb-3">Our Mission</h2>
            <p class="font-mono text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Our Mission is to open ourselves to God-seekers and to feed, nurture, and provide refuge for those in need.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white p-7 md:p-8 shadow-sm border-t-4 border-navy">
            <h2 class="eyebrow-navy mb-3">Our Purpose</h2>
            <p class="font-mono text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">Our Purpose is to care about others and their well-being with love.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-navy text-white p-7 md:p-8 mt-6">
          <h2 class="eyebrow-light text-white/75 mb-3">Our Values</h2>
          <p class="font-mono text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed text-white/85">Commitment, Community, Compassion, Diversity, Integrity, Joy, Lead, Love, Nurture, Pursuit of Faith and Social Consciousness.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="pastors-letter" class="bg-white py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8 prose-newsletter">
        <p class="eyebrow-crimson mb-3">A message from Rev. Neill D. McKay</p>
        <h2 class="display-lg mb-6">Friends and Family</h2>
        <p>It's OCTOBER, which means Pastor Neill gets to pull out my Halloween decorations and begin prepping for my personal favorite holiday of the year! The day when kids gather in small packs of merrymakers, all dressed up, knocking on total strangers' doors — who, with equal joy, give out sweets, treats, and other inventive treasures. One of my tricks in Alaska was to pass out little slips of scripture and a church invitation along with the candy — a simple way to reclaim and spread the Word of God on a day too many associate with paganism.</p>

        <h3 id="bbq-fundraiser" class="display-md text-navy mt-10 mb-4">October 11th BBQ Fundraiser</h3>
        <p class="font-semibold text-crimson">Hopewell Presbyterian Church · 5–7 PM</p>
        <p>On Sunday, October 11th, I will be hosting an Eastern North Carolina BBQ fundraiser at Hopewell Presbyterian Church to support my upcoming November mission trip to the Middle East. No tickets to buy; just come, bring friends, and give as God leads. The total cost of the trip will be $6,000. God will provide. Now this is Eastern North Carolina pulled pork, vinegar-based BBQ — not Texas brisket, certainly not South Carolina mustard-based — served buffet style with my special baked beans, Hopewell's prized coleslaw, and a dessert bake sale. Everything we do is outreach and community building.</p>
        <!-- SharePoint BBQ photographs can be placed here, beneath the fundraiser story. -->

        <h3 class="display-md text-navy mt-10 mb-4">The Mission Trip</h3>
        <p>This mission is an unapologetic focus on the suffering of Palestinian Christians, and not anti-Jewish. The approximately 1,200 Israelis killed during the cowardly October 7, 2023 Hamas attack were real people, and their grief is real. And yet the death toll since stands at nearly 75,000 Palestinians to approximately 2,000 Israelis — a 37 to 1 ratio that mirrors the historical pattern of this conflict. Israel controls all imports of food, fuel, and medicine into both Gaza and the West Bank which brings its own sense of oppression and insecurity. The call of the Gospel has always been to go where suffering is greatest, and voices are least heard. That is where we are going. Not to fight, denounce, or argue with anyone, but to let fellow Christians know they are seen.</p>
        <p>For clarity, Gaza was controlled by Hamas, which launched the October 7 attack. Hamas does not control the West Bank, which has been administered by the Palestinian Authority for decades — it is a different territory, a different government, and a different reality. The West Bank, with its rise in Jewish Settler Violence, is where we are going.</p>

        <h3 class="display-md text-navy mt-10 mb-4">Why Palestinian Christians?</h3>
        <p>Palestinian Christians are descendants of the original followers of Jesus Christ — connected to this land since his birth, ministry, death, and resurrection, and they have never left. Today, like so many dispossessed peoples, they are struggling and slowly dying out. Our mission is simple: to go and be present. To let them know they are seen, not forgotten, and beloved children of God.</p>
        <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-crimson pl-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-display text-2xl italic text-navy">“There is no longer Jew or Greek, slave or free, male or female — for you are all one in Christ Jesus.”</p>
          <cite class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-crimson not-italic">Galatians 3:28</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>Peace in Christ,<br /><strong>Rev. Neill D. McKay</strong></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="triangle-pattern py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8">
        <p class="eyebrow-crimson mb-2">Church News</p>
        <h2 class="display-xl mb-10">October at First Presbyterian</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 lg:gap-8">
          <article id="fun-fellowship" class="bg-white p-7 md:p-8 shadow-sm border-t-4 border-crimson prose-newsletter">
            <h3 class="display-md text-navy mb-4">Fun &amp; Fellowship</h3>
            <p>Sunday, October 18th, we will be assembling Sunshine Bags with goodies to bring smiles to our friends in need.</p>
            <p>Saturday, October 31st, is Pastor Neill’s Halloween Party at the Manse before Trick or Treating.</p>
            <!-- Sunshine Bags or fellowship photographs can be placed here. -->
          </article>
          <article id="worship-committee" class="bg-white p-7 md:p-8 shadow-sm prose-newsletter">
            <h3 class="display-md text-navy mb-4">Session Committee Highlights</h3>
            <p><strong>Worship Committee — Chairman: Karla Ritter</strong></p>
            <p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To provide for the worship of the people of God, including the preaching of the Word, the sharing of the Sacraments, and the music program.</p>
            <p><strong>Responsibilities:</strong> To establish the time, place, and appropriate Order of Worship for regular Sunday worship service, as well as special services of worship. To supervise the celebration of the Sacraments. To provide the proper setting for the conduct of public worship. To oversee the music program of the church. To provide for the continuing education of our congregation regarding the rich worship life of the Presbyterian and Reformed tradition.</p>
          </article>
          <article id="earth-care" class="bg-white p-7 md:p-8 shadow-sm md:col-span-2 prose-newsletter">
            <h3 class="display-md text-navy mb-4">Earth Care in Action</h3>
            <p>Dear Carers of the Earth,</p>
            <p>Time has come to take the next steps in our journey together! Please join us here at Holmes Camp &amp; Retreat Center on Oct. 10th for Earth Care in Action: Next steps for you and your congregation in stewarding creation.</p>
            <p>We are planning an engaging and interesting day together with learning sessions, farm tours and maybe even getting a little dirt under your nails. This along with lunch and all free!</p>
            <p>We would love to see you there and please forward this along to anyone else you think might be interested.</p>
            <p>If you have questions, to sign up for lunch or if you'll need a ride from the train station email me at <a href="mailto:pat@holmescamp.org">pat@holmescamp.org</a>.</p>
            <p>Yours, Pat Rossiter</p>
            <p>You may also contact Pastor Neill at <a href="mailto:NM2442@yahoo.com">NM2442@yahoo.com</a>.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="october-calendar" class="bg-navy text-white py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8">
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-end justify-between gap-4 mb-10">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow-light text-white/70 mb-2">Mark Your Calendar</p>
            <h2 class="display-xl text-white">October Calendar</h2>
          </div>
          <a href="/events" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-white/75 hover:text-white">All events →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="divide-y divide-white/15 border-y border-white/15">
          {calendarItems.map((item) => (
            <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-[10rem_1fr] gap-2 sm:gap-6 py-4">
              <span class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-white">{item.date}</span>
              <p class="font-mono text-sm leading-relaxed text-white/75">{item.detail}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="mt-10 border-l-4 border-crimson pl-5">
          <p class="eyebrow-light text-white/70 mb-1">Looking Ahead</p>
          <p class="font-mono text-sm text-white/85"><strong class="text-white">November 8</strong> — Westminster Guild Pie Contest</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white py-16 md:py-24">
      <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 lg:gap-12">
        <article id="food-pantry" class="prose-newsletter">
          <p class="eyebrow-crimson mb-2">Community Outreach</p>
          <h2 class="display-lg mb-4">Food Pantry</h2>
          <p>First Presbyterian Church will be on staff again in November. During a typical month, we serve more than 300 households — fully a 100% increase over several years ago. After a huge spike in usage, we have not seen the expected decrease since the COVID pandemic subsided.</p>
          <p>Local food drives and generous cash donations have helped limit what the Pantry has had to buy. Fortunately, supply chain issues have improved since the pandemic but the nonperishable items we can always use include canned fruit, veggies, tuna &amp; pork &amp; beans, cereal, pasta &amp; sauce, boxed mac &amp; cheese and boxed instant potatoes.</p>
          <p>When the Pantry does purchase food, it is generally from the Regional Food Bank here in Montgomery, where your donated dollars go farther than at retail supermarkets and other food suppliers.</p>
          <p>All donations are greatly appreciated, and volunteers are always needed! We’re open to serve clients on Tuesdays 10 AM–12 PM and Wednesdays 6:30–8 PM. We also pack nonperishable bags on Monday and Tuesday evenings from 6–7 PM so that bags are ready to go for serving clients the next day.</p>
          <p>If you can help out — even if it’s only a shift here and there — or if you have questions/comments/concerns, please call/text/email Robin McPhillips: <a href="tel:8454574194">845-457-4194</a> / <a href="tel:8459786457">845-978-6457</a>, <a href="mailto:rmcphil1120@gmail.com">rmcphil1120@gmail.com</a>. THANK YOU!</p>
        </article>
        <article id="nursery-school" class="bg-cream p-7 md:p-8 self-start prose-newsletter">
          <p class="eyebrow-crimson mb-2">Our Shared Spaces</p>
          <h2 class="display-lg mb-4">Welcome, Montgomery Nursery School!</h2>
          <p>Montgomery Nursery School is being warmly welcomed to Fellowship Hall!</p>
          <p>To accommodate the community's needs following the closure of Most Precious Blood School in Walden, Valley Central made two preschool classes available. Montgomery Nursery School (MNS) stepped up to the challenge and partnered with the First Presbyterian Church of Montgomery to transform Fellowship Hall into an active classroom space.</p>
          <h3 class="display-md text-navy mb-4">What This Means for Our Spaces</h3>
          <p>Because our building is now a bustling, shared environment, please keep the following adjustments in mind:</p>
          <p><strong>Coffee and Conversation:</strong> This gathering will now be a more closely attended, intimate time together to accommodate the new layout.</p>
          <p><strong>Shared Community Groups:</strong> In addition to MNS, our spaces are actively utilized by AA, Al-Anon, and Girl Scouts.</p>
          <p><strong>A Community Effort:</strong> With so many groups sharing the church, please show you care by cleaning up thoroughly after every event, so the space remains welcoming and ready for the next group.</p>
          <!-- Nursery school or Fellowship Hall photographs can be placed here. -->
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <GetNotified />
  <style>
    .prose-newsletter { color: #596174; font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.8; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .prose-newsletter p { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
    .prose-newsletter a { color: #af2333; text-decoration: underline; }
    @media print {
      :global(header), :global(footer), :global(#announcement-bar) { display: none !important; }
      main, section { background: white !important; color: black !important; }
      .text-white, .text-white\/70, .text-white\/75, .text-white\/85 { color: black !important; }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>